<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Artaxias I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 160 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Artashes I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Artaxias I was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxiad-dynasty">Artaxiad dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. A general serving the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a>, he declared himself king after the Seleucid defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-magnesia">Battle of Magnesia</a> in 190 BCE and united the Armenian-speaking lands, setting boundary stones that still survive. He founded the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artaxata">Artaxata</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> and reigned until about 160 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/artaxias-i.jpg" alt="Artaxias I" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leu Numismatik · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Artaxias_I.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
